artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a multiserver job queueing system with a fixed pool of $k$ identical servers. Jobs arrive according to a Poisson process of rate $\lambda$. Each arriving job belongs to one of finitely many classes; a class-$i$ job requires a fixed integer number $s_i \in \{1, 2, \ldots, k\}$ of servers, which it must occupy simultaneously and exclusively for the entire duration of its service. The service time of a class-$i$ job is an independent random variable with a known distribution (e.g., exponential with rate $\mu_i$). Jobs are served in first-come-first-served (FCFS) order, meaning a job at the head of the queue cannot start service until $s_i$ servers are simultaneously available; while it waits, no later job is allowed to bypass it, even if enough servers are free for the later job.

Assume the system has a finite waiting room of capacity $K$, where $0 < K < \infty$. An arriving job that finds $K$ jobs already waiting is lost (blocked and cleared); otherwise it joins the queue (or enters service immediately if enough servers are free at the head of the line).

Develop a rigorous analysis of this finite-capacity multiserver job model. In particular:
(a) characterize the stationary distribution (or its structural properties) of the joint process tracking the number of jobs of each class in service and in the queue;
(b) determine the blocking (loss) probability, mean number in system, and mean response time as functions of $\lambda$, $K$, $k$, the class mix, and the service-time distributions;
(c) establish monotonicity and comparison results with respect to $K$ (interpolating between the immediate-service / dropping model at $K=0$ and the infinite-buffer model as $K \to \infty$).

